.TH XD\-DISKMGMT 1 2026-10-18 XeonDesktop "XeonDesktop Manual"
.SH NAME
xd\-diskmgmt \- Disk Management
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY xd\-diskmgmt
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xd\-diskmgmt
is Windows XP's Disk Management, over UDisks2: the volume list above and
the disk map below, in XP's colours.
It initializes disks (GPT or MBR), creates, deletes, formats (quick, or
full: zeros written over the whole volume first; with a choice of
allocation unit size where the file system has one), extends and shrinks
partitions, changes labels and partition types, marks partitions active,
mounts and unmounts them, and ejects media.
.PP
Change Mount Point adds, changes and removes the folder a volume is
mounted in when the computer starts: an
.BR fstab (5)
entry, which UDisks2 writes, naming the volume by its UUID, with the
options
.B nofail
(the computer still starts without the disk) and
.B x\-mount.mkdir
(the folder is made if it's missing), and for FAT, exFAT and NTFS
.B uid
and
.B gid
so the files are yours.
The system's own entries can't be changed here.
.PP
Encrypted (LUKS) volumes are unlocked (asking for the passphrase),
locked (unmounted first) and given a new passphrase from the Action menu.
Format and the New Partition Wizard can encrypt a volume (LUKS2, or
whatever UDisks2 makes by default), the file system inside it.
Adding a startup mount point to an unlocked encrypted volume also adds
its
.BR crypttab (5)
entry, so it's unlocked when the computer starts: by default the
passphrase is asked for then; or it's kept in a key file that UDisks2
writes in
.IR /etc/luks\-keys ,
readable by root, and by anyone who can read the system disk, so the
volume is then only as safe as that disk.
Removing the last startup mount point removes the
.BR crypttab (5)
entry, and the key file with it.
.PP
Everything goes through UDisks2, which asks polkit, so
.BR xd\-polkit (1)
asks for a password; nothing runs as root here.
As Windows does for its system and boot volumes, what the running system
uses (\fI/\fR, \fI/boot\fR, \fI/usr\fR, swap in use...) can't be deleted,
formatted or shrunk.
Every destructive action asks first, naming the disk, the partition and
its size.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR udisksctl (1),
.BR xd\-admintools (1)
